  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 221 PM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nCurrent water vapor imagery and RAP analysis highlight a mid/upper \nwave tracking over the Northern Plains alongside a retrograding \nridge shifting over the Desert Southwest. A surface low resides in \nnorthern Iowa, and the associated cold front extends through \nsoutheast Kansas. Gusty north/northwest winds have been observed \nbehind this boundary so far today, and with these ushering \nrelatively cool air into the area, lows tonight should dip into the \n60s for most locations. Breezy north winds are once again expected \non Saturday primarily along and east of I-135 as a stout surface \nridge builds into the area. This will continue to advect cooler air \ninto the region, and subsequent afternoon highs in the 80s are \nforecast for most locations to begin the weekend. \n\nMid/long range models continue to indicate a slight warming trend \nthrough the beginning of next week as an upper ridge builds over the \nCentral Plains. Highs on Sunday will range from the middle 80s to \nlow 90s before some areas (especially in central Kansas) see highs \nreturning to the upper 90s on Monday. From here, near to slightly \nabove average temperatures are expected to remain in place areawide, \nthough per the prior discussion, dewpoints in the upper 50s and low \n60s should preclude headline-worthy heat indices through at least \nthe middle of the week.\n\nThe area should remain dry through Tuesday before long-range \nmodels highlight the next chances for rain as a mid/upper wave \ntraverses the Northern Plains. The associated cold front appears\nto stall out across Nebraska and Kansas which may result in on-\nand-off chances for showers and storms through the end of the \nweek. Details regarding timing and location remain a bit unclear\nthis far out, however, so stay tuned as additional information \nbecomes available.",
